Verdict: MAJOR

## Findings
- [major] The Resource Intelligence Summary still misses required issue-class sources for this issue’s actual label set. Live issue `#2460` carries both `cat:documentation` and `cat:harness`, and [docs/plans/README.md](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/docs/plans/README.md) says multi-class issues must use the union of required sources, including the durable-vs-transient boundary policy `#2209`, `config/agents/`, and `.claude/rules/`. The local plan’s `## Resource Intelligence Summary` cites `CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md` but not `#2209`, `config/agents/`, or `.claude/rules/`. That is still a retrieval/backlink gap for a documentation+harness contract plan, and it matters because this issue is defining routing/authority rules without verifying all upstream governance surfaces that the repo workflow makes mandatory.
- [minor] The threshold fix is not fully internally consistent. In `## Pseudocode` under `define data-placement scope explicitly`, the draft says “if a directory will exceed either 10 MB total or 1000 files,” while [docs/standards/DATA_PLACEMENT.md](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/docs/standards/DATA_PLACEMENT.md) uses the exact operational authority as `>= 10 MB` / `>= 1000 files` plus “If a directory will exceed either threshold (10 MB or 1000 files)”. Elsewhere in the draft the exact `>=` thresholds are preserved, but this pseudocode wording still leaves a strictness ambiguity in the implementation-facing section.

